// WHY: the Avra build system, lived inside @std/avrac because the
// build driver and the compiler ship together as one toolchain — same
// versioning, same release, same team. Modeled on cargo and
// go-build: fingerprint-keyed cache, dependency-graph-aware
// compilation, bottom-up parallel builds, progress UI. Used by any
// Avra project, including the bootstrap compiler itself.
//
// Layering:
//   * @std/avrac.parse / typeck / codegen / etc.   ← compile one file
//   * @std/avrac.build                              ← drive the build
//   * packages/cli (the bs2 binary)                 ← user surface
//
// Phases tracked under epic forge-crafting-intepreters-a1el:
//   Phase 1: fingerprint + cache the existing single-unit build
//   Phase 2: split @std/avrac as a separately-cached unit
//   Phase 3: per-module units, metadata files, parallel workers
//   Phase 4: progress bars, --timings, lockfile, profiles
//
// Public surface lives in submodules; this file is the namespace
// root. Consumers reach machinery via:
//   use @std.avrac.build.fingerprint.{fp_for_unit, ...}

mod fingerprint
mod cache
mod driver
mod graph
